/*Q29. Write a Java program that displays the number of characters, lines, and words in a text. Your program should read the input until it
encounters the character -1 in a new line.
Input Format:
• The input consists of multiple lines of text.
• Each line is read as a complete string.
• Input continues until the user enters -1 on a separate line.
Output Format:
• The first output line contains a positive integer representing the number of characters in that text.
• The second output line contains a positive integer representing the number of lines in that text.
• The third output line contains a positive integer representing the number of words in that text.
Input example:
a
il
Output example:
2
1
I
Note:
• The count of characters includes all visible characters as well as the newline characters (\n) at the end of each line (except for the
terminating -1 line).

*/
import java.util.Scanner;
 
public class TextStats {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
        int chars = 0, lines = 0, words = 0;
        while (sc.hasNextLine()) {
            String line = sc.nextLine();
            if (line.equals("-1")) break;
            lines++;
            chars += line.length() + 1;            // +1 for the newline character
            String t = line.trim();
            if (!t.isEmpty()) words += t.split("\\s+").length;
        }
        System.out.println(chars);
        System.out.println(lines);
        System.out.println(words);
        sc.close();
    }
}
